          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %strong affine space
            or
            %strong affine linear space
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            that has forgotten its origin. An
            %strong affine linear map
            (a morphism of affine spaces) is a linear map (a morphism of vector spaces) that need not preserve the origin.
          %p
            Note that the ‘linear functions’ of elementary algebra —the total functions whose graphs are lines— are in fact (precisely)
            %em affine
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            to itself. (Similarly, the ‘linear relations’ —the relations whose graphs are lines— are precisely the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+space"} projective
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          %p
            Alternatively, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mi n
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^n k
            (affine line, affine plane, etc.) over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            refers to, depending on context, the set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi k
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+algebra"} polynomial algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[x_1, \ldots, x_n]
            – these definitions coinciding if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraically+closed+field"} algebraically closed field
            – and typically considered as equipped with relevant extra structure such as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zariski+topology"} Zariski topology
            or, going even further, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+ringed+space"} locally ringed space
            structure adhering to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+variety"} affine variety
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+scheme"} affine scheme
            corresponding to the polynomial algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[x_1, \ldots, x_n]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mi n
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^n k
            is a setting in which the study of loci of polynomial equations, i.e. definable sets in the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebras"} commutative algebras
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            Most of this article concerns affine spaces in the sense of vector spaces that have forgotten their origins or identities; the algebraic geometry sense is very briefly touched upon in the section
            = succeed "." do
              %em Affine spaces as model spaces
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#summary Summary
          %p The definition of affine space can be made precise in various (equivalent) ways. We give a name to some of the definitions for later reference.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                An affine space is simply a vector space, but with different morphisms; an affine linear map is a function that is the difference between a linear map and a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+function"} constant function
            %li
              %p
                An affine space is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                equipped with an equivalence class of vector space structures, where two vector space structures are considered equivalent if the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+function"} identity function
                is affine linear as a map from one structure to the other; whether a map between affine spaces is affine linear is independent of the representative vector space structures.
            %li
              %p
                An affine space is a set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                together with a vector space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                and an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                of (the additive group or
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/translation+group"} translation group
                of)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                that makes
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                into a
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsor"} torsor
                (over the point); an affine linear map is a
                = succeed "-equivariant" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
